WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.917
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Hi.

00:00:00.917 --> 00:00:01.950
I'm Priyanka Vergadia.

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:05.400
And in this video, we will
see what are hyperparameters,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.370
how to tune them, and set
up a hyperparameter tuning

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.640
job in Vertex AI.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.940
All right, so what
is a hyperparameter?

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.180
Well, a training application
handles three categories

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.710
of data as it trains the model.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:24.930
The input data, which is
also called training data,

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:30.340
contains the features that the
model uses to make predictions.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.400
Now, but this data
is never directly

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.080
part of the model
architecture itself.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.350
The second type of data
is model parameters.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.850
These are the variables that
our ML model or technique

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.110
uses to adjust the data.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.640
For example, a deep
neural network, or DNN,

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.180
is composed of processing
nodes or neurons,

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.820
each with an operation
performed on the data

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.170
as it travels
through the network.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.710
Now, when our DNN is
trained, each node

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:03.180
has a weight value that tells
our model how much impact it

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.610
has on the final prediction.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.460
Now, they are what distinguishes
our particular model

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.430
from other models of the
same architecture trained

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:12.970
on different data.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.350
Now, the third type of
data is hyperparameters.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.450
And these are the
variables that govern

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.410
the training process itself.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:24.240
For example, a part of
designing a deep neural network

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.180
is deciding how
many hidden layers

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.110
you will want between the
input and the output to be,

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:35.010
and how many nodes each
hidden layer would use.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.710
Like here I have two,
then three, then three,

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.900
but this could be
any number of nodes.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.740
These variables are not directly
related to the training data,

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.350
and they are the configuration
variables for your model.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:50.160
Now, when it comes
to model parameters,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.700
they can change with
the training job.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.730
But hyperparameters
are usually going

00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:00.600
to stay constant, which means,
in our deep neural network

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:05.070
model example, finding the
right number of hidden layers

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:08.400
means choosing a value of
the number of hidden layers,

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.030
training a model, evaluating
it with those values,

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.610
and then trying it again
with a different value.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.660
Now, if we wanted to get the
best type of parameter settings

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.560
manually, it could get pretty
tedious pretty quickly.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.880
That's where Vertex AI
hyperparameter tuning comes in.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.740
It is an automated process
of tuning hyperparameter jobs

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.340
as you're running your
multiple trials of the training

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.420
application with values of
the chosen hyperparameters set

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.790
within the limits
that you specify.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:45.300
And to select hyperparameters
that give the best performance,

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.660
Vertex AI uses the
Bayesian optimization

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.250
for hyperparameter tuning.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.530
If you're interested
in the math behind it,

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.960
I've included a link below
to learn more about it.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.000
With that, let's
jump into the console

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.150
and train a
hyperparameter tuning job.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.460
Here I am in the
Google Cloud console.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.820
First, you need to enable
Compute Engine and Container

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.380
Registry APIs.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:11.190
In this demo, I'm training and
tuning an image classification

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.370
model trained on the
horses or humans data set

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.800
from TensorFlow data sets.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.890
We will need a notebook to
containerize our training

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.120
application code.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.100
Since I'm creating a TensorFlow
model in this example,

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.180
I'm going to select a
TensorFlow Enterprise 2.5

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.490
notebook without a GPU.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:32.790
But you can train a model
built with any framework using

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.370
custom or pre-built
containers, so select

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.230
the notebook accordingly.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.560
I already created one here,
so let's just hop into that.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.340
In the terminal, I created a
folder called Horses or Humans,

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:47.160
and inside that folder,
I've created a Docker file.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:50.700
This Docker file uses deep
learning container TensorFlow

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.490
Enterprise 2.5 GPU Docker image.

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:56.370
And the deep learning
containers on Google Cloud

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.220
actually come with many common
machine learning and data

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.620
science frameworks
that are pre-installed.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.450
We're also installing the
Hypertune Library here,

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:07.230
which we will use later
to report the metrics we

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.930
want to optimize on Vertex AI.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.360
After downloading the
image, the Docker file

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.750
sets up the entry point
for the training code.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:19.769
Then, I created the Trainer
folder with task.py file

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:23.140
and added the code for
training and tuning the model.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.370
Let's take a deeper
look at this code now.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:29.490
There are a few components
that are specific to using

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.710
hyperparameter tuning service.

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.830
The script imports
the Hypertune Library.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:38.490
We installed this library in the
Docker file we created earlier.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:43.350
And the function, get_args,
defines a command line argument

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:46.200
for each hyperparameter
that you want to tune.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.660
In this example,
the hyperparameters

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.420
that will be tuned
are the learning rate,

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.270
the momentum value
in the optimizer,

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:58.500
and the number of neurons in the
last hidden layer of the model.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.220
But feel free to experiment
with others, as well.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:03.830
The value passed
in these arguments

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.400
is then used to set the
corresponding parameters

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.450
in the code.

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:11.480
At the end of main function,
the Hypertune Library

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.750
is used to define the
metric you want to optimize.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.900
In TensorFlow, declares
model.fit method

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.820
returns a history object.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:22.760
The history.history
attribute is a record

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:25.580
of training loss
values and metrics

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:27.770
values at successive epochs.

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:31.040
If you pass validation
data to model.fit,

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.610
the history.history attribute
will include validation loss

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.840
and metrics values as well.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.280
If you want the hyperparameter
tuning service to discover

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.150
the values that maximize the
model's validation accuracy,

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:47.210
you define the metric
as the last entry,

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:52.460
or NUM_EPOCHS as minus 1
of the val_accuracy list.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:56.660
Then pass this metric to
an instance of hypertune.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:58.730
You can pick whatever
string you like

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:01.220
for the hyperparameter
metric tag.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:03.410
I'm setting it to accuracy here.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:05.870
But you will need to
use the same string

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:10.430
again later when you kick off
the hyperparameter tuning job.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:12.920
Now we're ready to
build the container.

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:17.480
Set the project ID, image
URI, build the container

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:19.430
using the image URI.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.920
Once the container
is built, push it

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.930
to the container registry.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:26.540
And then next, let's
head into the Vertex AI

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:30.470
console and create a model to
run our hyperparameter tuning

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:31.850
job.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:35.600
To configure a training
job, select No Manage Data

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:39.170
Set, Custom Training,
give the model a name,

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:41.900
and then select the custom
container that we just

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.030
created from our notebook.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.700
Notice that here, I'm
using Custom Training

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:50.540
via Custom Container on Google
Cloud Container Registry.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:53.900
But you can also run a
hyperparameter tuning job

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:56.420
with the pre-built containers.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:59.660
In the next step, enable
hyperparameter tuning,

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:03.920
add learning rate, which would
be double, min and max values,

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.450
and log scaling.

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:08.090
Another parameter,
which is momentum,

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:11.480
type would be double,
minimum and maximum values,

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:12.650
linear scaling.

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:14.840
And third
hyperparameter would be

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:19.040
on number of neurons,
discrete type, and of value,

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.570
and no scaling for this one.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:25.070
Next, we need to provide the
metric we want to optimize

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:26.450
as well as the goal.

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:29.000
This should be the same
as our hyperparameter

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.940
metric tag we set in the
training application.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:37.330
It was set to accuracy, and
our goal is to maximize it.

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:39.940
The Vertex AI hyperparameter
tuning service

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:43.150
will run multiple trials
of our training application

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:44.440
with these values.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:48.490
You will need to put an upper
bound on the number of trials

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:49.780
this service will run.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:53.170
For demo purposes,
I'm adding 15 here.

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:56.050
More trials generally
lead to better results,

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:58.630
but there will be a point
of diminishing returns

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:01.150
after which
additional trials have

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:02.890
little or no effect
on the metric

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:04.430
you're trying to optimize.

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:07.510
So it's always a best practice
to start with a smaller

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:10.480
number of trials and get
a sense of how impactful

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.230
your chosen hyperparameters
are before scaling up

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:16.450
to a larger number of trials.

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:19.750
Now you'll also need to set
an upper bound on the number

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:21.550
of parallel trials.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:23.620
Here, I'm setting this to three.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:25.990
Increasing the number
of parallel trials

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:29.050
will reduce the amount of
time the hyperparameter tuning

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:31.570
job takes to actually run.

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:34.840
However, it can reduce the
effectiveness of the job

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:35.650
overall.

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:38.530
This is because the
default tuning strategy

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:41.890
uses results of previous
trials to inform

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:45.260
the assignment of values
in the subsequent trials.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:47.830
So if you run too many
trials in parallel,

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:50.710
there will be trials that
start without the benefit

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:54.400
of the results from the
trial that are still running.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:58.120
Now the last step is to
select the search algorithm.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:00.760
Now grid search is a very
traditional technique

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:02.650
for implementing
hyperparameters.

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:06.740
It's a brute-force, combines
all the combinations.

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:10.540
Now random search randomly
samples the search space

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:14.170
and evaluates the sets
for specified probability

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:15.190
distribution.

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:18.520
For example, instead of trying
to check all the 100,000

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:22.960
samples, we can check 1,000
random sample parameters.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:25.670
We will select default
search algorithm here,

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:29.920
which will use Google Vizier to
perform Bayesian optimization

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:31.510
for hyperparameter tuning.

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:33.580
To learn more about
how this works,

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:35.920
see the documentation below.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:39.550
In Compute and Pricing, leave
the selected region as is

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:42.880
and configure Worker Pool
zero with a machine type,

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:45.370
accelerator, and disk size.

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:49.120
Now note here that selecting
GPU is totally optional--

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:51.010
hyperparameter
tuning job will just

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:55.240
take a little longer to complete
if you do not use a GPU.

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:56.860
Then start the training.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.140
It should take about
45 minutes to train

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:02.080
with the demo parameters
that I've selected here.

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:04.360
And when it's
finished, you will be

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:07.630
able to click on the job
name and see the results

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:09.490
of the tuning trials.

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:11.290
From the results
of our trial, we

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:15.070
can see here that setting
the learning rate to 3.9,

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:20.980
momentum to 2.8, and neurons
to 128 results in the highest

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:23.260
validation accuracy.

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:25.390
In this video,
we've learned what

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:29.230
is hyperparameter tuning,
how Vertex AI helps automate

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:31.150
the hyperparameter
tuning process,

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:33.700
and, then, we launched a
hyperparameter tuning job

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:37.180
for training code in Custom
Container via the UI.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:41.140
If you would like to do the
same using Vertex Python SDK,

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:42.940
I have linked the codelab below.

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:46.100
Give it a try, let me know how
it goes, and for any questions,

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:49.350
reach out to me on
Twitter @pvergadia.

